cocotb testbench for the Chipalooza harness digital subsystem
=============================================================

Setup (once), following the same pattern as lctime:

	python3 -m venv venv
	./venv/bin/pip install -r requirements.txt

Run:

	PATH=$PWD/venv/bin:$PATH make
	PATH=$PWD/venv/bin:$PATH make COCOTB_TEST_MODULES=test_spi
	PATH=$PWD/venv/bin:$PATH make COCOTB_TESTCASE=test_nop_command
	PATH=$PWD/venv/bin:$PATH make WAVES=1		# dump a VCD

VERILOG_SOURCES names only dut.v, because the rest of the design
arrives through `include and make cannot see an include.  The Makefile
adds verilog/rtl/*.v as an explicit prerequisite of sim.vvp so that an
RTL edit does rebuild.  Anything OUTSIDE verilog/rtl --- the PDK models,
or the behavioural analog models in dependencies/ --- is still not
tracked, so "make clean" first after editing those.

One WARNING line is expected in a clean run:

	WARNING: digital_top.bandgap ...: bandgap bias out of range

It comes from test_bias_range_check_rejects_a_wrong_setting, which
deliberately mis-sets a trim to prove the check is not vacuous.  Any
OTHER bias warning means a test is running an analog block outside the
conditions it was characterised at, and the number that block produces
is not trustworthy.

PDK_ROOT/PDK must point at the IHP PDK so that sim_defs.v can find the
SRAM and standard-cell models.  They default to $(HOME)/gits and
ihp-sg13cmos5l respectively.

What this replaces
------------------
verilog/rtl/digital_tb.v is a linear smoke script:  it has 32 $display
calls but only five of them compare against an expected value, and none
of them assert, so a wrong result prints and the run still exits 0.  It
is useful for eyeballing a waveform and is kept for that.  These tests
assert, and fail the build when they should.


=============================================================
TESTS CURRENTLY EXERCISED
=============================================================

test_spi.py --- SPI protocol and register file
	test_identity_registers
		Manufacturer ID (0x567), product ID (0x18) and SPI
		status read back their hard-coded values.
	test_mask_rev_passthrough
		mask_rev_in appears byte for byte at 0x04..0x07.
	test_register_write_readback
		Eight writable registers hold what was written.
	test_address_autoincrement
		A write stream walks consecutive addresses, and so does
		a read stream.
	test_csb_resets_spi
		A transfer abandoned after the address byte leaves the
		register unchanged and the interface still usable.
	test_nop_command
		Command 0x00 does not write.

test_sram.py --- pattern memory over SPI
	test_sram_write_read
		Four bytes written and read back.
	test_sram_autoincrement_across_256
		The SPI address phase is 8 bits, so only auto-increment
		reaches above 255.  Writes 0xFC..0x103 and reads back.
	test_sram_high_addresses_are_distinct
		0x100 is a different location from 0x00, i.e. the
		address carries rather than wrapping.
	test_sram_walking_ones
		Walking ones and walking zeros, so every data bit is
		exercised independently.
	test_sram_read_does_not_disturb
		Repeated reads return the same contents.
	test_register_access_after_sram_access
		sram_ena clears, so register reads work after an SRAM
		stream rather than continuing to return memory.

test_clocking.py --- claims the timing constraints assert but cannot prove
	test_sram_clock_sources_are_exclusive
		An SRAM access command stops the sequencer.  This is the
		premise behind -logically_exclusive on sram_clk_c and
		sram_clk_s;  without it the SRAM could be clocked by SCK
		while the sequencer runs on clk.
	test_sram_clock_follows_selected_source
		sram_clk tracks SCK when stopped and clk when running.
	test_seq_strobe_is_not_a_clock
		Back-to-back sequencer commands in one CSB session both
		take effect.  Guards the seq_load restructure;  the old
		data-generated seq_strobe clock dropped the second.
	test_loop_mode_tracks_command
		Looping vs single-shot select different loop_mode.
	test_digital_reset_clears_sequencer
		Command 0x04 clears the sequencer with clk stopped,
		which is what loc_reset being asynchronous is for.


test_project_control.py --- per-slot selection, input latch, gated clock
	test_select_is_one_hot
		Every one of the 18 addresses enables exactly one slot.
		An x here means proj_addr is undriven, which is what the
		bit_index fix in digital_top.v addressed.
	test_slot_zero_selects_nothing
		Address 0 is the diagnostic address and matches no slot.
	test_addresses_above_18_select_nothing
		19, 20 and 31 are unassigned;  proj_addr is a truncated
		localparam, so an off-by-one would show up here.
	test_unselected_slots_see_no_enables
		No unselected slot has enable, 3.3V or 1.2V asserted.
	test_dig_in_latch_is_transparent_then_holds
		Program the routing, raise dig_ena so the whole word
		arrives at once, lower it, change the routing underneath,
		and confirm the project still sees the held value.
		FOUND THE RESET_B INVERSION (see BUGS FOUND below).
	test_dig_in_latch_clears_when_deselected
		Deselecting resets a slot's latch, so a project cannot
		keep a stale word from when it was last configured.
	test_only_the_selected_slot_sees_the_bus
		One project gets the pattern, seventeen get zero.
	test_proj_clk_runs_only_on_the_selected_slot
		The clock gate passes clk to one slot;  the other 17 stay
		low.  A gate stuck open would load a clock tree that was
		not built for it.
	test_proj_clk_has_no_glitch_when_selection_changes
		Selecting a slot with clk parked HIGH emits no edge.
		This is what the lgcp_1 is for and what a plain
		"select & clk" gets wrong;  clk stopped high is
		explicitly permitted.
	test_proj_clk_stops_cleanly_on_deselect
		The mirror case:  deselecting does not leave a project's
		clock frozen high.
	test_proj_reset_is_synchronised_and_per_slot
		Command 0x04 reaches only the selected project, and only
		once the clock has run, since reset_sync is two stages
		deep.  Releases when CSB clears dig_reset.
	test_dig_out_daisy_chain_is_continuous
		Both chains relay their zero seed through all 18 slots
		and arrive resolvable, not x.  A break or a misindexed
		slice in dbus_vec_left/right shows up as x here.

test_analog.py --- power gating, bias routing, analog switches, bandgap
	test_power_gate_reaches_only_selected_slot
		3.3 V and 1.2 V arrive at the selected slot at their
		nominal values and NaN everywhere else.
	test_power_gates_independent
		All four combinations of the two gate enables.
	test_deselecting_removes_power
		Selecting another slot removes power from the previous.
	test_power_is_off_after_reset
		No slot comes up powered.
	test_bias_switches_route_to_selected_slot
		ibias0, ibias1 and vbias reach one slot only.
	test_bias_switches_independent
		The three are separately controlled.
	test_vbias_follows_voltgen
		The bias delivered to a project tracks voltgen: zero with
		no bandgap reference, non-zero with one, and monotonic in
		the selector.  No absolute voltage is asserted.
	test_voltgen_disabled_is_zero_not_floating
		A disabled generator is a connected 0 V, NOT NaN.  The
		distinction is the whole point of the sentinel.
	test_analog_bus_enables_are_one_hot
		Only the selected slot's four bus switches are on.
	test_analog_bus_lines_independent
		analog_bus_ena selects the four lines individually.
	test_analog_bus_is_not_connected_to_any_slot_after_reset
		Nothing reaches a project from the shared pins on reset.
	test_project_zero_asserted_only_for_slot_zero
		project_zero follows proj_sel == 0, including out of
		reset, where 0 is the default.
	test_diagnostic_switches_drive_the_shared_pins
		Selecting project 0 puts the internal biases on the four
		shared pins, and any other selection closes all four ---
		otherwise an internal bias would be shorted onto a pin a
		project is using.
	test_bandgap_off_is_zero_and_on_is_plausible
		0 V disabled;  enabled, inside a sanity band.  NOT a spec
		check: see the note on the trim equation below.
	test_bandgap_trim_is_monotonic_and_saturates
		Raising the code never lowers the output, the
		thermometer decode has exactly N bits set for code N, and
		codes above 16 are all the same setting.
	test_pads_are_disconnected_by_default
		An undriven shared pad reads NaN, not 0 V.
	test_pad_voltage_reaches_only_the_selected_slot
		End to end: pad -> resolving mux -> per-slot switch ->
		project.  All four pads carry different voltages so a
		transposition between bus lines cannot pass.
	test_pad_is_gated_by_its_own_bus_enable
		A pad reaches a project only while that line's enable is
		set.
	test_diagnostic_drive_takes_precedence_over_the_pad
		With project 0 selected the internal driver wins over an
		external one, and releasing the pad does not disturb the
		diagnostic measurement.

test_bias.py --- the current bias generator and what it feeds
	Driven from harness.BIAS_DEFAULTS, the documented working
	settings, rather than from values invented for the test:
		0x18 = 9   biasgen enable | stabilize with bandgap
		0x1D = 12  bandgap sink2 = 1 unit, sink1 = 4 units
		0x1E = 36  voltgen sink2 = 4 units, sink1 = 4 units
		0x1F = 4   voltgen source = 4 units
	Every trim is an integer count of unit currents;  the unit is
	250 nA under these settings, so 1 uA is a count of 4 placed in
	whichever bit field the register assigns.
	test_bias_defaults_produce_the_documented_currents
		All five internal biases come out at nominal.  If a bit
		field moves in housekeeping.v, or a documented value
		changes, the mismatch lands here.
	test_bias_range_checks_pass_with_the_documented_settings
		The bandgap and voltgen both accept what they are given.
		FOUND THE BANDGAP BIAS CROSSING (see BUGS FOUND below).
	test_bias_range_check_rejects_a_wrong_setting
		The range check is not vacuous:  halving a trim fails it.
	test_biasgen_disabled_zeroes_every_bias
		A disabled biasgen sources and sinks nothing.  Zero, not
		NaN:  the block is present and off, not absent.
	test_idac_outputs_scale_with_the_unit_current
		idac1 and idac2 are integer counts of 250 nA.
	test_idac_current_reaches_a_project_unclamped
		The current a project receives is the current the iDAC
		sourced.  Guards CURRENT_MODE on the bias switches.
	test_bias_polarity_is_preserved_through_the_switches
		Sinks stay negative and sources positive at the
		destination, which is why biasgen signs them.
	test_bias_registers_read_back
		The documented values survive a write/read round trip.


=============================================================
BUGS FOUND BY THESE TESTS
=============================================================

user_project_control.v, the dig_ena input latch.  The 24 dlhrq_1 cells
had .RESET_B(~select).  RESET_B is ACTIVE LOW, so the inversion held the
latch in reset on whichever slot was selected --- that project's 24
inputs were pinned to zero and it could never receive anything from the
shared bus --- while all seventeen unselected slots had their reset
released and went transparent on dig_ena, latching the bus instead.
Exactly backwards, and invisible to static timing, which by design does
not cover dig_in -> proj_dig_in at all.  Now .RESET_B(select).
NOTE:  this changes the user_project_control netlist, so the block needs
re-synthesis before the result is signed off.

digital_top.v, the bandgap bias connections.  The two blocks number
their bias pins by different conventions:

    biasgen2   bandgap_sink1 is the 1 uA sink   (3-bit trim)
               bandgap_sink2 is the 250 nA sink (2-bit trim)
    bandgap    ibias1_250n   wants 250 nA
               ibias2_1      wants 1 uA

They had been paired up by ordinal, which delivered 1 uA where 250 nA
was wanted and 250 nA where 1 uA was wanted --- a clean factor of four
the wrong way on both pins, with the documented register settings
correctly programmed.  Now crossed to match the magnitudes.  Caught by
the bias range check added to the bandgap model, which is what that
check was for.  Behavioural model only:  nothing in the digital netlist
changes, but the corresponding connection in the analog schematic is
worth confirming against this.


=============================================================
MODEL LIMITATIONS, NOT RTL BUGS
=============================================================

Representing an analog node as one real is the root of all of these:
the number has no units, no impedance and no direction, so each model
has to be told out of band what it is carrying.  Verilog-A is what would
make them go away, by carrying branch voltage and current together.
What is left after the fixes recorded above:

1.  The slot wrappers are empty, so proj_dig_out is undriven.  The
    dig_out daisy chain can only be checked for continuity of the zero
    seed, not for data injected by a selected project.

2.  The dedicated per-slot analog pins are still tied to a NaN constant
    (pad_not_modelled in digital_top).  Only the four SHARED pins are
    exported.  Wiring up the dedicated pins means 40 more scalar real
    ports, which is worth doing only if something is going to drive
    them.

3.  Contention on a shared pin is resolved in favour of the on-chip
    driver rather than by output impedance.  Defined, but not physical.

RESOLVED, and how, since they bound what earlier tests could assert:

  - The four shared analog nets had no driver, so with a slot selected
    they read NaN and nothing could be routed to a project.  digital_top
    now exports them as analog_pinN_in / analog_pinN_out.  They are two
    ports per pad because a real has no high-impedance state, iverilog
    rejects a real inout, and a real cannot have two drivers.  The
    diagnostic switches feed a resolving mux instead of driving the net
    directly.  BEWARE:  an undriven "input real" reads 0.0, which is a
    legitimate voltage, so harness.reset drives all four to NaN.

  - analog_pswitch_small clamped its output up to PMOS_FLOOR = 0.8 on
    nets carrying a CURRENT, turning a 250 nA bias into 0.8.  The model
    now takes a CURRENT_MODE parameter that disables the voltage floor,
    and the four instances on the ibias distribution set it.  The device
    is the same either way;  only the meaning of the value changes.

  - biasgen2 ignored its "ena" input.  Both unit currents are now gated
    by it, so all seven outputs go to zero together when the block is
    disabled --- and to NaN, not zero, if the enable is unknown, since
    nothing downstream could tell "off" from "never driven" otherwise.

  - The bandgap and voltgen models had a "to do" asking for a check that
    the bias currents handed to them are in range, their behaviour being
    uncharacterised away from nominal.  Both now carry one.  It is a
    readable flag (bias_ok) plus a warning while the block is enabled,
    not a clamp and not $error:  clamping would hide a misconnection and
    $error would stop a deliberate sweep.  NaN fails the check, which is
    intended.


=============================================================
NOT YET COVERED
=============================================================

Add to this list as gaps are found;  it doubles as the to-do list.
The feature lists below were read out of the RTL headers, so they may
name things the main document does not yet describe.

Sequencer (sequencer.v).  seq_mode, register 0x10, three bits:
	0  binary up count			NOT TESTED
	1  binary down count			NOT TESTED
	2  grayscale up count			NOT TESTED
	3  grayscale down count			NOT TESTED
	4  pseudorandom (LFSR)			NOT TESTED
	5  constant zero (sequencer off)	NOT TESTED
	6  constant one (sequencer off)		NOT TESTED
	- start/stop counts (0x12..0x15) bounding the sequence
	- seq_prescaler (0x0E) dividing the interval
	- single-shot terminating vs looping wrapping
	- lfsr.v itself:  sequence length, and no lockup at zero

Pattern generator (pattern.v).  sram_mode, register 0x11, two bits:
	0  increment at the sequencer clock rate	NOT TESTED
	1  variable rate;  alternate memory locations hold
	   the delay before the next step			NOT TESTED
	2  increment at sequencer end			NOT TESTED
	- pat_stop (0x16/0x17) as the recycle point
	- the documented one-shot convention:  a value of all ones in
	  the location just past the end of the sequence
	- pat_prescaler (0x0F), and its multiplication with the
	  variable time step in mode 1

Router / crossbar (router.v).  Wholly untested.
	- the 24 input-route nibbles (0x20..0x37):  constant 0,
	  constant 1, sequencer output, SRAM output, and pass-through
	  from each of the 12 I/O pins
	- the 12 output-route nibbles (0x38..0x43)
	- io_oe direction control and the 6/6, 8/4, 10/2 splits
	- sram_monitor (0x4C), strobe_monitor and seq_monitor (0x4D)
	- io_in -> dbus_out and dbus_in -> io_out feedthrough

Project control (user_project_control.v).  NOW COVERED by
test_project_control.py, except:
	- the dig_out daisy chain carrying real data.  Only continuity
	  of the zero seed is checkable while the slot wrappers are
	  empty (model limitation 3 above), so the OR of the two chains
	  at the housekeeping end is still unproven for a 1
	- the routing itself:  which dbus_out bit lands on which
	  proj_dig_in bit is checked as a 24-bit word, but the word is
	  built out of constants, so a transposition inside the router
	  would not show.  That belongs with the router tests below

Power and bias configuration (0x18..0x1F, 0x51, 0x52).  NOW COVERED by
test_analog.py and test_bias.py at the documented settings.  Still open:
	- biasgen's coarse / fine unit-current selection.  Only the
	  default (both clear, so a 250 nA unit) is exercised;  the
	  50 nA, 1 uA and 333 nA units are not, and neither is
	  ref_sel_vbg = 0, where the source unit mirrors the sink
	- voltgen_high, which switches the resistor divider from
	  vbg/4 to vbg/3
	- the bandgap trim EQUATION.  Deliberately not asserted: the
	  model's curve is linearized between endpoints pending a
	  curve-fit, and the resistances are still being tuned, so a
	  test against 0.96688 + j * 0.0203 would only confirm that the
	  placeholder is still there.  Once the curve is final, add a
	  value check;  the monotonicity and saturation tests already
	  written should survive it unchanged
	- an out-of-bounds assertion on the bias currents handed to the
	  bandgap and voltgen models.  Both carry a "to do" saying
	  their behaviour is uncharacterised away from -250 nA / -1 uA,
	  so a testbench that quietly supplies something else is
	  measuring nothing

Gate level.  Everything here runs against RTL.  A gate-level mode using
verilog/gl with SDF back-annotation is what would make this signoff
grade rather than RTL grade.  Note that the PDK cell models' specify
blocks are currently discarded (-gno-specify in the Makefile) because
iverilog cannot parse their "ifnone" edge-sensitive paths;  that needs
solving, or a different simulator, before SDF would mean anything.


=============================================================
HARDWARE BEHAVIOUR THE TESTS ENCODE
=============================================================

SRAM reads lag by one byte.  The SRAM cannot latch the full address by
the next SCK edge, so the byte shifted out during transfer k+1 holds the
contents of address k, and the first byte out is a dummy (X after
reset).  harness.py's sram_read discards it, so callers get exactly the
bytes they asked for.  digital_tb.v documents the same quirk above its
own read.

The register map in harness.py was read out of the odata mux in
housekeeping.v rather than copied from the documentation, so that a
divergence between RTL and document shows up as a failing test instead
of as a test that agrees with the wrong thing.
